ou will have access to  so here I'm thinking xanthan gum skim  milk powder and cornstarch the protein  content and the milk powder and just I'm  not totally sure how xanthan gum works  I'm not going to try to [ __ ] an  explanation and then with cornstarch per  unit of volume it just absorbs an  assload of water to help keep their from  being free water in the mixture that can  float around and form large ice crystals  so  here's how you make a super super good  homemade ice cream base you start with a  pint of cream and a cup of milk you put  that on the stove over medium low heat  you go ahead and throw in a quarter  teaspoon of xanthan gum you can probably  find that at most stores it's pretty  common now a lot of vegans use xanthan  gum as an egg substitute so as more  stores have become have started catering  to the to a vegan lifestyle xanthan gum  has become more ubiquitous you're giving  me a face they just eat plain xanthan  gum as a no no no like not as like  scrambled eggs but if you're oh like it  if if like a brownie recipe calls for an  egg okay you would use xanthan gum I was  like do that that is a bridge too far  you know no John I yes I understand that  face now so so xanthan gum is more  forgiving than a lot of other  stabilizers which is why I go with that  so you're gonna throw it in the milk and  cream mixture as it's coming up to  temperature make sure you whisk it in  really well so it fully dissolves and  you just keep that over medium-low heat  keep an eye on the temperature  eventually you want that to get up to a  hundred and eighty degrees Fahrenheit  which is some number of degrees Celsius  that's part of the metric system that I  can't do conversions with super quickly  yet so 180 degrees Fahrenheit that's  what you're aiming for while that's  coming up to temperature what you want  to do is crack some eggs get eight eight  egg yolks you can save the whites for  whatever you would want to use egg  whites for scrambled egg whites egg  w